    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today with a high-floor lineup, anchored by **Corbin Carroll** (77.03) and **James Wood** (74.74), who emerge as our top plays. **James Wood** specifically benefits from an elite xwOBA profile and a highly favorable park factor (+14%) against Kyle Freeland. Meanwhile, **Corbin Carroll** remains a priority start; despite a slight dynamic platoon disadvantage against Jeffrey Springs, his elite sprint speed and elevated position in the batting order ensure he remains an essential catalyst for our stolen base production. **Tyler Soderstrom** also clocks in as a core play at 70.82, leveraging a massive +19% SP skill advantage against Mitch Bratt, making him one of our most efficient sources of power today.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today was excluding **Austin Riley** (59.57) from the starting nine. While his power profile is formidable, the optimizer leaned toward **Josh Naylor** (62.78) and **Cole Young** (58.36) due to better situational platoon alignments and SP skill matchups. **Austin Riley** was ultimately benched because his current projected output against JP Sears failed to overcome the cumulative efficiency advantages provided by our middle infield and utility depth. We are also monitoring the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Gabriel Moreno**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Brice Turang**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Cole Young**, **Isaac Paredes**, **Henry Bolte**, **Corbin Carroll**, **James Wood**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Seiya Suzuki**, and **Josh Naylor** closely; should the confirmed lineups shift these players significantly down the order, we will be forced to adjust our aggression levels to protect our daily game caps.\n\nBeyond the benching of **Austin Riley**, we see significant upside in **Isaac Paredes** (68.21), who benefits from a favorable platoon advantage against Janson Junk, and **Brice Turang** (66.10). **Brice Turang** is particularly intriguing; while he faces a tough arm in Freddy Peralta, the combination of a slow opposing delivery and his own elite speed makes him a high-probability candidate to swipe a bag, maintaining his value despite the tough matchup. Keep an eye on wind conditions for **Zach McKinstry** and **Seiya Suzuki**, as both received a +5% boost from wind-out projections, adding a layer of home run potential that elevates them over the high-floor but low-ceiling alternatives on our bench like **Mickey Moniak** or **Kyle Karros**.",
